    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d481">

                <pb facs="0519" n="401" sameAs="frus:frus1929v03/pg401"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">812.00Sonora/804</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        the Secretary of Labor</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Davis</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1929-04-24">April 24, 1929</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to enclose for your
                    information a copy of a telegram, dated April 15, 1929, from Mr. Gerzayn
                        Ugarte,<anchor n="56" xml:id="d481-f1"/> concerning Antonio I. Villarreal
                    and Raul Madero, stated to be generals of the Mexican insurrectionist forces,
                    who recently took refuge in the United States and who it is said are under
                    consideration for deportation from this country. In this connection reference is
                    made to the Department’s letter of April 22, 1929 regarding Raul Madero.<anchor
                        n="56" xml:id="d481-f2"/></p>
                <p>I may add that Mr. Ugarte is understood to be an agent of the Mexican
                    insurrectionists and that the Department does not deem it expedient to
                    acknowledge his communication inasmuch as this Government does not treat with
                    the agents of the present insurrection in Mexico.</p>
                <p>It is requested that this Department be consulted prior to the taking of any
                    action which may be contemplated by your Department now <pb facs="0520" n="402"
                        xml:id="pg402"/> or at some future date, which may affect the status of
                    Mexican insurrectionists in the United States or their departure from this
                        country.<anchor n="57" xml:id="d481-f3"/></p>
                <p>I have [etc.]</p>
                <closer>For the Secretary of State:<lb/><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Wilbur
                            J. Carr</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d481-f1" n="56" xml:id="d481-f1-content">
                <p>Not printed.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d481-f2" n="56" xml:id="d481-f2-content">
                <p>Not printed.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d481-f3" n="57" xml:id="d481-f3-content">
                <p>A similar request was made in Department’s letter of the same date (not printed),
                    regarding Nicolas Perez, stated to be a Mexican Senator, and Vincente Ramos,
                    said to be Secretary of the Permanent Commission of the Chihuahua State
                    Legislature, who desired to remain in the United States (812.00 Sonora/803).</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
